            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Closest Alternative: Folding Pad:</span> <a href="#walkingpad-c2">WalkingPad C2</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this brand guide">
          <h2>In this brand guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#is-cybergoing-legit">Is Cybergoing Legit?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#lineup">The Models</a></li>
            <li><a href="#t10-in-detail">The T10</a></li>
            <li><a href="#bosch-motor">The Bosch Motor</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline-and-speed">The 7% Incline</a></li>
            <li><a href="#app-and-remote">App and Remote</a></li>
            <li><a href="#brand-store">Cybergoing’s Store</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty-and-returns">Warranty and Returns</a></li>
            <li><a href="#ownership">Care and Problems</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-it-suits">Who It Suits</a></li>
            <li><a href="#alternatives">Alternatives</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Cybergoing is a legitimate but very new walking pad brand, and its T10, at about $142 on Amazon on 25
            November 2026, is the model most buyers should consider: a 300 lb, 4 mph under-desk pad with a fixed 7% incline.
            The "Powered by Bosch" line comes with no power figure, and the warranty is 12 months.</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>T10:</strong> about $142, flat under-desk pad with a 7% incline, 0.6 to 4 mph, 300 lb, 15 by
            36-inch belt, app and remote, per the Amazon listing.</li>
            <li><strong>T12:</strong> about $170, adds a handle bar and a top speed of 6.2 mph; the listing gives no
            weight limit.</li>
            <li><strong>T16:</strong> about $218, handrails, 0.6 to 7.6 mph, 330 lb and a longer 39.7-inch belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty:</strong> 12 months on the main product and 6 months on "battery and core components",
            per Cybergoing's refund policy (checked 25 November 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns:</strong> 30 days on Cybergoing's own store, buyer pays return shipping unless the machine
            is faulty; on Amazon, Amazon's terms and the listing's seller apply.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Nothing on this page was tested hands-on. It is built from the Amazon listings as they read on 25 November
            2026 and from Cybergoing's own store and policy pages. If you are still deciding whether you want a walking pad
            at all, or which kind, start with our <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-cybergoing-legit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is Cybergoing Legit? Who Makes and Sells It</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Cybergoing is a real company selling real machines, and nothing we found suggests a scam. It is,
            however, new to treadmills, and that is the main thing to weigh.</strong> It runs its own online store at
            cybergoing.com, sells its <a href="/best-walking-pads-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pads on Amazon</a> under the CYBERGOING brand, and publishes a contact address,
            a support email and written return, shipping and warranty policies.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">An e-scooter brand that added walking pads</h3>
          <p>
            Cybergoing's own About Us page (checked 25 November 2026) does not mention treadmills at all. It describes the
            company as a personal mobility brand whose "flagship product" is the CyberPioneer Series e-scooter. Its warranty
            page is written for scooters too: it opens by saying that "all brand-new Cybergoing escooters" are covered for a
            year, and one line says valid claims will be processed "through Hiboy", which is the name of a different
            scooter brand and reads like text carried over from a template. Neither point is a red flag on its own, but
            together they say that the treadmill business is a recent addition and the paperwork has not caught up.
          </p>
          <p>
            The store's product records point the same way. Its three treadmills were added in 2026: a pad called simply
            "Treadmill for Home" in May, the T16 in July and the T12 in October. A brand with six months of walking pad
            sales has no track record of how its machines hold up after two or three years, and no reviewer can tell you
            that yet.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where the company is</h3>
          <p>
            The contact page gives an office address in Nanshan District, Shenzhen, China, the email
            support@cybergoing.com, a promise to respond within 24 hours and customer service hours of Monday to Friday, 9
            AM to 6 PM China Standard Time. That is late evening to early morning on the US East Coast, so expect email
            rather than a same-day phone conversation. The store's public Shopify profile lists Kowloon Bay, Hong Kong. There
            is no US phone line on the policy pages we could read.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">So is it safe to buy?</h3>
          <p>
            Buying a Cybergoing is no riskier than buying any other new budget walking pad brand, provided you buy where a
            return is easy. On Amazon that means checking who the listing says ships and sells the machine before you order,
            because that decides who handles a return in the first weeks. After that, the warranty runs through Cybergoing's
            support email, and the quality of that support is the part nobody can yet vouch for.
          </p>
