          <ul>
            <li><strong>UREVO Smart Walking Pad</strong> costs slightly less than the T10 and gives you a longer belt and a
            published motor figure, but no incline and a lower 265 lb rating. Our <a href="/urevo-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">UREVO guide</a> covers the brand's range and
            its warranty terms.</li>
            <li><strong>WalkingPad C2</strong> costs more than twice as much and carries less weight, but folds in half for
            storage and comes from one of the longest-established walking pad brands. Our <a href="/walkingpad-z1-review/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">WalkingPad Z1 review</a> covers a sibling
            model.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are not set on a brand at all, our roundup of the <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads</a> compares the field by
            role: budget, incline, capacity, folding and quiet. A Cybergoing makes most sense when you specifically want an
            incline and a 300 lb rating for under $150; it makes less sense when a flat deck, a longer belt or a longer
            record would suit you as well.
          </p>
