        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Cybergoing Walking Pad? Avoid These 4 Mistakes</h2>
          <p>Most problems with a Cybergoing come from reading more into the listing than it says. These four are worth avoiding.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Reading "Powered by Bosch" as a power rating</h3>
              <p>The listings name Bosch but give no horsepower, continuous or peak. Do not compare a Cybergoing with a pad that publishes a continuous rating as if the Bosch name settled it; judge on capacity, belt and warranty instead.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming the T12 carries 300 lb</h3>
              <p>The T10 says 300 lb and the T16 330 lb, but the T12 listing gives no weight limit at all. Get the figure from the seller in writing before you order a T12, especially if you are a heavier walker.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a fixed incline when you want flat walking</h3>
              <p>Every Cybergoing has a fixed 7% incline, and the listings do not say clearly whether the deck can sit flat. If you want to walk flat some days, confirm it with the seller or buy a flat pad such as the UREVO instead.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming 12 months covers the motor</h3>
              <p>Cybergoing’s policy gives 12 months on the main product but 6 months on undefined "core components". Ask whether the motor and controller are covered for the full year, and keep your order number and the reply.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>Our verdict on the brand</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Cybergoing is a legitimate but very new walking pad brand, and its T10 at about $142 is
          the one to buy.</strong> It suits slow daily desk walking on a built-in 7% slope for users well inside its 300 lb
          rating, with a remote and app for speed. The T12 adds a handle and jogging speed, and the T16 adds handrails, a
          longer belt and 330 lb, at prices that start to overlap with proper folding treadmills.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treat "Powered by Bosch" as a brand name rather than a power rating, assume 12 months of warranty with a question
          mark over the motor, and buy on Amazon for the lower price and easier returns. If you want a flat deck or a
          longer record, compare the <a href="/urevo-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">UREVO</a> range or our <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best walking pads</a> first.
          </p>
        </div>

